## Layout

{% if domain %}
The `{{ domain }}` domain's modules live directly under `src/`:

```text
src/
  commands.rs
  events.rs
  queries.rs
  services.rs
  states.rs
  types.rs
  views.rs         # only when the domain declares views
  shared/          # only when the crate needs shared items
```

Outside the domain modules:

- `actors` — actors and their permissions.
- `action` — the `Action` trait implemented by every command input and query
  params struct, returning the permission that `Actor` checks.
- `shared` — unassigned items plus the types and events pulled in from other
  domains, when there are any.
{% else %}
Every domain owns a folder with its commands, events, queries, services,
states, views and types:

```text
src/
  domains/
    <domain>/
      commands.rs
      events.rs
      queries.rs
      services.rs
      states.rs
      types.rs
      views.rs         # only when the spec declares views
      mod.rs
```

Outside the domains:

- `actors` — actors and their permissions.
- `action` — the `Action` trait implemented by every command input and query
  params struct, returning the permission that `Actor` checks.
- `shared` — items that were not assigned to a domain, when there are any.
{% endif %}

`DomainEvent` and `Event` come from the [`event-store`](https://github.com/0m3kk/backend-kit)
crate; import them from there directly. Event modules use
`event_store::tag_value` directly as well.

Service methods return `impl Future<Output = ...> + Send`; implement them with
`async fn`. The traits are not object-safe.

## States and views

States and views share the params + projected-fields shape, but have different
lifecycles.

### States

States are in-memory decision models that are rebuilt on every rehydration.
Their `Reducer` trait methods are synchronous and fold an event into the state.
Each state gets a `new(params...)` factory; a state with no params also
implements `Default` so it can be constructed as an empty projection:

```rust
let state = YourState::new(account_id);
```

### Views

Views are persisted PostgreSQL tables. A view does **not** get a `new()`
factory or a `Default` implementation; an empty row is not a valid table row.
For each view the generator emits:

- a row struct for query results;
- a `ViewDefinition` type and a `<VIEW>_VIEW` constant with the table name and
  primary-key columns;
- a `<View>Store` trait with one `on_*` method per event type, receiving the
  typed event and its sequence position. The application layer implements these
  methods with SQL;
- a migration with the table, primary key, declared indexes, and the standard
  `last_event_position`, `created_at`, and `updated_at` columns. Migrations are
  written to the `--migrations` directory.

A domain gets a `views.rs` module only when it declares View cards; specs
without views do not emit an empty module.

The event types and tags that feed a projection are recorded in its doc
comments.

Every file in this crate starts with a header stating that it was generated by
`em-codegen` and must not be edited by hand. Change the specification or the
templates and regenerate instead.

## Domain events

There is no central event enum. Each event struct implements the
`DomainEvent` trait from the `event-store` crate, which knows its type name and
tags and can produce a stored event:

```rust
use event_store::{DomainEvent, Event};

fn persist<E: DomainEvent>(event: &E) -> Result<Event, serde_json::Error> {
    event.to_store_event()
}
```

